: 0, "edit_calls": 1, "ended_on_rejection": false, "error": null, "exec_mode": "local", "failure_class": "solved", "failure_reason": null, "faults_served": 0, "final_answer_complete": true, "final_answer_present": true, "final_answer_source": "json", "fresh_subagents": 0, "harness": "omp", "harness_version": "11.9.0 (/workspace/pi-check/cache2/bin/omp/20663d0e0d0452dc3e41b38370e1e8f6e7036579/omp)", "harness_version_source": "host", "host": "grok-bot-vm-412615941", "image_digest": null, "last_activity_age_s": null, "list_has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": false, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": 1000000, "model_max_tokens": 128000, "output_tail": "Shell is mangling indentation; use a one-liner.\nI fixed the bug, which was in `ledger/currency.py`, in `to_cents`. All 4 tests in `tests/test_totals.py` and the one in `tests/test_report.py` now pass. pytest isn't installed here, so I ran each test function directly with `python3`.\n\n**Cause:** `int(amount * 100)` chops off the decimal part of a float calculation that isn't exact. `19.99 * 100` comes out as `1998.9999\u2026`, which became `1998` instead of `1999`. `0.29` became `28` (\u00d73 = 84, not 87), and `4.35` became `434`. Every total built on these was off by a cent or more.\n\n**Fix:** convert the amount through `Decimal(str(amount))` and round half up to whole cents. Converting through `str` means `19.99` is treated as exactly 19.99, not as the slightly-off float value. Rounding half up matches how `discounts.py` and `tax.py` already round.\n\n```python\ncents = Decimal(str(amount)) * 100\nreturn int(cents.quantize(Decimal(1), rounding=ROUND_HALF_UP))\n```\n\nIf a price has more than two decimal places, it is now rounded to the nearest cent instead of cut off: `0.125` gives `13`, where before it gave `12`. If you'd rather reject those prices outright, that would need an explicit check. Callers passing `Decimal` or `int` prices work unchanged.", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "permission_reject